{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b036c90bb96d2195","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Major. The strategy builds\n13\tadministrator-facing chargeback reporting on top of per-project GPU-hour metering\n14\tdata delivered by the in-flight strategy RHAISTRAT-9990 (external dependency,\n15\tschema owned there and not final until that strategy lands).\n16\t\n17\t## Strategy Parsing Notes\n18\t\n19\t- The strategy provides HLRs (HLR-1..HLR-4) and an Affected Components list\n20\t  (Dashboard, Distributed Workloads) but **no Impacted Teams table, no\n21\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no NFRs**.\n22\t  Owner teams were inferred from `.context/architecture-context/` (RHOAI\n23\t  Dashboard owns Dashboard; Distributed Workloads owns Distributed Workloads);\n24\t  epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs. Recorded as health\n25\t  warnings below.\n26\t- No Staff Engineer Input section is present — no overrides to log.\n27\t- Both affected components exist in the platform (architecture context files\n28\t  `dashboard.md` and `distributed-workloads.md` under\n29\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`), so no onboarding\n30\t  chain (repo/license/konflux) is needed; both are Konflux-onboarded already.\n31\t\n32\t## Open Question Handling (no Investigation epic)\n33\t\n34\tThe strategy's single open question states that the metering data schema\n35\tfinalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands and that report implementation\n36\t\"carries that external timing risk.\" Applying the decision rule — does the\n37\tanswer change which downstream epics exist or what they do? — **no**: the\n38\treporting view, export/API, quota comparison, and documentation epics exist and\n39\tdeliver the same capability regardless of the schema's final field layout; the\n40\tschema shapes field mapping inside those epics (an implementation detail the\n41\tconsuming team resolves), and the timing is a schedule risk controlled by an\n42\texternal party. The unknown is therefore NOT a genuine unknown warranting an\n43\tInvestigation epic. It is modeled as:\n44\t\n45\t- an **external precondition** recorded in the Risks/Notes of the consuming\n46\t  epics (RHAISTRAT-9990 is a separate strategy — there is no epic in this\n47\t  decomposition to draw a DAG edge to), and\n48\t- **acceptance criteria on the consuming epics** phrased at the engineering\n49\t  completion point they control (reports computed from the metering data as\n50\t  delivered).\n51\t\n52\tThis also means no conditional branches: the outcome cannot add or remove epics\n53\tin this plan.\n54\t\n55\t## Epic List\n56\t\n57\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n58\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n59\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Expose per-project Kueue quota data for chargeback reporting | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n60\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Administrator GPU-hour consumption reports with CSV export and reporting API | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P0 |\n61\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Admin view of current-month consumption against Kueue quota | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Document report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | Documentation | P1 |\n63\t\n64\tEpic count: 4 (no branch epics).\n65\t\n66\t## Dependency DAG\n67\t\n68\t```mermaid\n69\tgraph TD\n70\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 quota data exposure]\n71\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 consumption reports + export/API]\n72\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 consumption vs quota view]\n73\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 documentation]\n74\t    E001 --> E003\n75\t    E002 --> E003\n76\t    E001 --> E004\n77\t    E002 --> E004\n78\t    E003 --> E004\n79\t```\n80\t\n81\tCritical path length: 3 (e.g., E002 → E003 → E004).\n82\t\n83\t## DAG Justification\n84\t\n85\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n86\t|------|------|-----------|\n87\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumed) | The quota comparison view builds against the per-project quota data surface E001 produces; the Dashboard owns no data plane and reads component-provided data. |\n88\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (shared surface) | HLR-3 renders the same per-project consumption data the reports epic surfaces; serializing avoids two Dashboard epics building the same consumption-reporting surface independently. |\n89\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation is blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n90\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation is blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n91\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation is blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n92\t| (no edge) RHAISTRAT-9990 → E002/E003 | External precondition, not an edge | The metering strategy is a separate strategy outside this decomposition; its timing risk is recorded as a risk/AC on the consuming epics, never as a DAG edge to a nonexistent epic. |\n93\t| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 14 (independent repos/artifacts) | Quota exposure (Kueue side) and consumption reporting (Dashboard, metering data) share no artifacts — they run in parallel. |\n94\t\n95\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n96\t\n97\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epic | Notes |\n98\t|-----|----------|------------------------|------|-------|\n99\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges | RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Direct cover. |\n100\t| HLR-2 | P1 | CSV export and stable API for finance tooling | RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | **(bundled)** — CSV export and the retrieval API are presentation modes of the same per-project report surface and data access built for HLR-1; splitting would force a second epic to rebuild the same surface and data pipeline, so HLR-2 is not separately schedulable. Epic priority remains P0 from HLR-1. |\n101\t| HLR-3 | P1 | Dashboard admin view shows current-month consumption against Kueue quota | RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Split from E002 (not bundled): the quota comparison is a distinct, deferrable feature that additionally depends on the Distributed Workloads quota data exposure — bundling it into the P0 reports epic would serialize P0 reporting behind Distributed Workloads quota work. E003 consumes the reporting surface built by E002 (E002 → E003 edge) so the surface is not duplicated. The quota-data half of HLR-3 is enabled by RHAISTRAT-9018-E001. |\n102\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation covers report semantics and export integration | RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | docs-authoring epic; priority P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority Major → P1), not inherited from HLR-4's P2. |\n103\t\n104\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No redundant/overlapping\n105\tHLR pairs were found (HLR-1 and HLR-3 are distinct views — time-range reports\n106\tvs current-month quota comparison — over related but differently-sourced data).\n107\t\n108\t## Health Warnings\n109\t\n110\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n111\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no NFRs. Teams\n112\t   were inferred from architecture context; epic ACs were derived from HLRs.\n113\t   The refinement record for this strategy should be asked to supply ACs and\n114\t   NFRs before implementation sign-off.\n115\t2. **External timing risk (scope trap)**: E002 and E003 consume the metering\n116\t   data schema owned by RHAISTRAT-9990, which is not final until that strategy\n117\t   lands. This is an externally controlled schedule constraint — recorded as a\n118\t   risk on both epics with ACs written at the engineering completion point the\n119\t   epics control; it cannot be compressed by this decomposition. If\n120\t   RHAISTRAT-9990's final schema cannot support per-project attribution over\n121\t   time ranges, E002/E003 require rework (rewrite), and HLR-4's semantics\n122\t   section changes accordingly.\n123\t3. **Ambiguous API placement**: the strategy does not state where the \"stable\n124\t   API for finance tooling\" (HLR-2) lives. Dashboard architecture context says\n125\t   the Dashboard owns no data plane and its interfaces are the Kubernetes API;\n126\t   a finance-facing reporting API is therefore an assumption — it is placed\n127\t   with the Dashboard reporting epic (the reporting surface this strategy\n128\t   builds). If review locates the API in a backend component instead, E002\n129\t   splits into a Dashboard view epic plus a backend API epic.\n130\t4. **Affected Components listed without change descriptions**: the strategy\n131\t   lists components only. The quota-data exposure epic for Distributed\n132\t   Workloads is inferred from HLR-3's \"against each project's Kueue quota\" —\n133\t   Kueue quota is owned by Distributed Workloads per architecture context.\n134\t5. **Docs priority vs HLR priority**: E004 is P1 (docs-authoring exception,\n135\t   strategy priority Major) while its only HLR (HLR-4) is P2 — expected per\n136\t   the docs-authoring priority exception, noted here for reviewers.\n137\t\n138\t## Tiered Delivery\n139\t\n140\tThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-9990 metering data, uncertain timing) was\n141\tevaluated for tiered delivery. Partial value IS deliverable without it:\n142\t\n143\t- **Tier 1 (independent of the metering strategy)**:\n144\t  RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 — per-project Kueue quota data exposure. Built entirely\n145\t  on Kueue data available today; de-risks the quota-comparison integration and\n146\t  can start immediately.\n147\t- **Tier 2 (gated on the external metering strategy delivering its final\n148\t  schema)**: RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 and RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 — all\n149\t  consumption-dependent reporting work. The gate is an external precondition\n150\t  (risk + AC on each epic), not a DAG edge. RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 (docs)\n151\t  follows all implementations per Rule 11.\n152\t\n153\tComponent mapping notes: all epic `component` values (Dashboard, Distributed\n154\tWorkloads, Documentation) are verbatim canonical names from\n155\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`; no non-obvious mappings were needed.\n156\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9632d07af5dd8eab","agent_id":"ac1711a44088525e6","session_id":"552a4708-ccc9-4336-802d-f1ef32ac46ea","uuid":"9351976b-2348-41e0-9723-a438fd04927a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:29:42.059Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b036c90bb96d2195","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
